---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  seoTitle={seoTitle}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="skills"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Data analyst vs software engineer salary in India: on Indeed, software engineers average about Rs 8.79 lakh
    a year and data analysts about Rs 6.28 lakh, so engineers lead by roughly 40%, but city, employer and skills can
    shrink or reverse that gap.</strong>
    The employer you join and the result you own can matter as much as the job title, and routes where you sell a skill directly can grow your income furthest. Choosing with real numbers also shows which skills to
    build first, and those skills and proof are the start of a <strong>high-income skill portfolio</strong>.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>On Indeed, engineers average more (Rs 8.79 vs Rs 6.28 lakh), with the biggest gaps in Bengaluru and Gurgaon and a small one in Noida. Other salary sites do not agree with each other, so read 40% as a direction, not a promise.</li>
      <li>At the first job the order can reverse: Indeed lists entry-level analysts at Rs 5.15 lakh and entry-level engineers at Rs 4.01 lakh. One likely reason is that IT services fresher packages sit near Rs 3 to 4 lakh.</li>
      <li>Income can grow furthest where you sell a skill directly. We rate an engineer who ships tools or takes contract work, and a data engineer, as Scalable, an analyst with deep industry knowledge as Scalable with a business layer, and a fixed-band services job or reporting-only analyst work as Not so scalable.</li>
      <li><strong>The expensive mistake is choosing from one average or one screenshot. Compare two offers in writing, then build the skill that makes either role hard to replace.</strong></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    Not sure which lane suits you? <a href="/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/career-coaching-for-it-professionals/">Career coaching for IT professionals</a>
    helps you make the decisions that unlock high income opportunities, from which offer to take to which skill to build first.
    More salary and skill comparisons sit in the <a href="/blog/skills/">career skills guides</a>.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="short-answer">Data analyst vs software engineer salary in India: the short answer</h2>

  <p>
    On the one source that lists both jobs the same way, software engineers earn more. Analysts can start ahead at the very first job,
    and analysts at the right employers can out-earn the average engineer.
  </p>

  <p>
    Pay range checked on 16 November 2026: Indeed India shows data analysts between about Rs 3.10 and Rs 12.70 lakh a year
    (average Rs 6.28 lakh). For software engineers it shows an average of Rs 8.79 lakh, and AmbitionBox shows a typical range of
    Rs 2.8 to Rs 16 lakh (page dated February 2025). Pay varies by city, employer and skill.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      "Engineers earn more" is true as one source's average and misleading as advice. An engineer on a fixed services package and
      an analyst at a product company are not in the same pay lane, whatever their titles say.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    To see how you handle numbers and logic problems, which both jobs test, try the free <a href="/services/assessments/analytical-and-critical-thinking-skills-assessment/">analytical and critical thinking skills assessment</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="this-week">Do this week</h2>
  <p>Averages do not pick your job. Use this week to compare real postings and build one thing you can show.</p>
  <div class="week-box">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Day 1.</strong> Open three live postings for data analyst and three for software engineer in your city. Write the pay line where it is given and the three skills that repeat.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 2.</strong> If you hold an offer, write it as fixed pay, variable pay and notice period. Compare two offers on fixed pay first.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 3 to 6.</strong> Build one small piece of work: an analyst can make a dashboard from a public dataset, and an engineer can ship a small working tool. Write the result in three lines.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 7.</strong> Ask one person in each role what the first year felt like and what they wish they had learned earlier.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="week-box__note">The salary gap is a direction, not a promise. Employer type and skills move pay more than the title does.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="numbers">The salary numbers, same source side by side</h2>

  <p>
    The cleanest comparison uses one source for both jobs. These are Indeed India figures, checked on 16 November 2026.
    The engineer page was last updated in May 2026 and the analyst page in October 2026.
  </p>

  <p>A CTC is not monthly pay. Turn either figure into in-hand pay with the <a href="/tools/ctc-to-in-hand-salary-calculator/">CTC to in-hand salary calculator</a>.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Measure</th>
          <th>Software engineer</th>
          <th>Data analyst</th>
          <th>Gap</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {sameSourceRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Measure"><strong>{item.measure}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Software engineer">{item.swe}</td>
            <td data-label="Data analyst">{item.da}</td>
            <td data-label="Gap">{item.gap}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Gaps are our subtraction of the two Indeed averages. Averages describe a pool of reported salaries, not what you will be offered.
    Verify against live postings before a financial decision.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sources-differ">Why salary sites give different numbers for the same job</h2>

  <p>
    Each site counts different people on different dates. Read these as four views of the same market, not as four answers.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Source</th>
          <th>What it shows</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {otherSourceRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Source"><strong>{item.source}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What it shows">{item.reading}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Glassdoor puts the average engineer near Rs 6 lakh while Indeed says Rs 8.79 lakh. That gap inside one job is
    about as big as the gap between the two jobs, so one headline number should never decide your career.
  </p>

  <h2 id="first-job">The first job: where analysts can start ahead</h2>

  <p>
    At the very first job the order can reverse. On Indeed, the entry-level analyst average (Rs 5.15 lakh) is higher than the entry-level
    engineer average (Rs 4.01 lakh).
  </p>

  <p>
    One likely reason is the fresher engineer pool. Business Today reported in February 2026 that a TCS trainee package was
    around Rs 3.36 lakh, with Infosys, Wipro and Accenture entry packages largely in the Rs 3 to 4 lakh range.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">When the analyst offer wins at the start</div>
      <ul>
        <li>The analyst offer is from a bank, insurer, retail chain or GCC (the India office of a global company, called a global capability centre).</li>
        <li>The engineer offer is a fixed IT services trainee package.</li>
        <li>You enjoy business questions more than building software.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">When the engineer offer wins at the start</div>
      <ul>
        <li>The engineer offer is from a product company or GCC.</li>
        <li>The analyst role is mostly manual reporting with no growth step.</li>
        <li>You can show a deployed project and solve coding problems under time.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="after-first-job">Pay after the first job</h2>

