  <h2 id="engineer-lift">What lifts an engineer's pay</h2>

  <p>
    The engineer side has the wider pay spread, so the same skill can land at very different salaries. These moves change which end you land on.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Moves that raise pay</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Move from an IT services firm to a GCC or product company once you have one deployed project and clear interview answers.</li>
        <li>Go deep in one area employers pay for, such as backend systems, cloud or data pipelines, instead of touching five tools.</li>
        <li>Own one feature or service from start to finish, with tests and a note on what broke and how you fixed it.</li>
        <li>Learn to explain a trade-off in plain words to a manager who does not code.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Moves that look good but rarely help</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Collecting certificates without a shipped project.</li>
        <li>Changing your title inside the same fixed-band company.</li>
        <li>Chasing every new framework in turn.</li>
        <li>Quoting one salary website's number in a negotiation.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ceiling">Where each salary stalls, and what lifts it</h2>

  <div class="mistakes-list">
