  <h2 id="who-pays">Who pays for a data analyst or a software engineer, and how they judge you</h2>

  <p>
    A data analyst is paid by a business team that needs a number it can trust: a bank, insurer, retailer, hospital or a firm that sells
    analytics to others. They judge you by one clear analysis on messy data and whether you can explain it to someone who decides.
    The first basic skill is to turn a spreadsheet question into a one-page answer.
  </p>

  <p>
    A software engineer is paid by a product company, a GCC or an IT services client, who judge working code, tests and how you solve problems in an interview.
    The first basic skill is one project a hiring manager can run without your help.
    For the side route, a small business owner pays an analyst for a dashboard or an engineer for a tool, so say who you help, show one finished sample, ask for referrals and name your price calmly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and pay for both roles</h2>

  <p>
    Both jobs lose the routine layer first. For engineers that is boilerplate (repeated template code) and test scaffolding. For analysts it is first-pass queries and standard charts.
  </p>

  <p>
    A PrepNPlaced report on October 2026 postings, covered by Careers360, found hiring leaning toward experienced people, with only 4.3% of tech postings at entry or junior level.
  </p>

  <p>
    The pay that holds goes to people who decide which metric matters, check that the data is right, and own a system or a result.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Start now</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Use AI to draft SQL or code, then test every answer against real data.</li>
        <li>Write down each time the AI was wrong and how you caught it.</li>
        <li>Pick one real problem at work or in a project and finish it end to end.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Build toward</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Owning metric definitions and data quality for a team.</li>
        <li>Designing the pipeline or system that AI tools run on.</li>
        <li>Judging the output of AI tools for a business decision.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    For the engineer side of this risk, read
    <a href="/blog/ai-future/software-developer-career-future-with-ai-india/">software developer career future with AI in India</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">Check before you commit</h2>

  <p>
    The four questions test whether this pay lane suits your life, and the three proofs test whether you can do the work before you spend money on it.
  </p>

  <h3 class="check-h3">The four questions</h3>

