  <h3 class="check-h3">The three proofs</h3>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <p class="gates-block__title">
      Pass all three proofs in your chosen lane before you pay for a course or leave a job.
    </p>
    <div class="gates-row">
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Proof 1</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Show it</span>
        <p>
          For the analyst lane, take a messy public dataset and produce one finding with a chart and a short written recommendation.
          For the engineer lane, ship one small working app with tests and a short readme.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Proof 2</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Say it</span>
        <p>
          Explain your project in two minutes with no notes: the problem, what you did, what you would change. If you can only
          explain tools, you cannot yet defend a salary.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Proof 3</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Get it checked</span>
        <p>
          Show it to a working analyst or engineer and ask whether it would get you shortlisted at their company. Use that answer to
          decide your next step.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="money-check">Money check before you pay</h2>

  <p>
    Our strongest suggestion: keep any course or degree fee close to a tenth of your family's education budget, and ask for strong, course-specific evidence before going past a fifth.
    Free YouTube lessons and cheap Udemy or Coursera courses need no such check. A university certificate on the same topic can cost far more than the free version, so start with the free one.
  </p>

  <p>
    An illustration, not a real fee: if a family sets aside Rs 4 lakh for all of a student's learning, about Rs 40,000 is our suggested limit for one course, and Rs 80,000 is where extra evidence is needed.
    The rest pays for what actually raises pay: a laptop that runs your projects, internet, datasets to practise on, interview preparation and a reserve for the gap between jobs.
  </p>

  <p>
    Test the topic free first with YouTube, official documentation and one low-priced course. If you still want a paid course, check the instructor's recent work, the last update, graded projects, refund terms and what past learners actually got.
  </p>

